1
00:00:01,125 --> 00:00:02,627
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:03,085 --> 00:00:04,837
Listen, there's this poker game
we've got going on.

3
00:00:04,962 --> 00:00:06,714
- You want in?
- Maybe. When?

4
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:09,842
Let me see. Thursday night.
You're going to play poker.

5
00:00:10,426 --> 00:00:12,011
Hey. Teresa.
Glad you made it.

6
00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:13,971
- This is district attorney Don Wolcott.
- Hi.

7
00:00:14,096 --> 00:00:15,431
FBI Bureau Director
Charles Bailey.

8
00:00:15,556 --> 00:00:17,350
- Sir.
- Senator Eileen Dawkins.

9
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:18,809
State Senator.

10
00:00:18,935 --> 00:00:21,270
It's a pretty high-class crowd.
I wish you would have warned me.

11
00:00:21,479 --> 00:00:22,772
Oh, I think you know
this guy, right?

12
00:00:23,147 --> 00:00:25,441
Teresa. I didn't take you
for a gambler.

13
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:27,527
It's not gambling if you know
you're gonna win.

14
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:35,952
Ace of hearts.

15
00:00:41,457 --> 00:00:42,500
Not me.

16
00:00:44,335 --> 00:00:45,836
Action's on you, Judge.

17
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:48,673
Well...

18
00:00:49,715 --> 00:00:50,758
$500.

19
00:00:52,927 --> 00:00:53,970
Dealer folds.

20
00:00:54,428 --> 00:00:55,888
That's $500 more to you, Bertram.

21
00:01:00,226 --> 00:01:01,561
Raise it $1,000.

22
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:08,651
You're bluffing.

23
00:01:10,611 --> 00:01:11,654
All in.

24
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:27,878
I love playing with you, Gale.
I can read you like a kids' book.

25
00:01:28,254 --> 00:01:31,591
- Yeah, yeah. Just pick up your chips.
- "Goodnight Moon."

26
00:01:33,342 --> 00:01:35,177
Ay, yi, yi.

27
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:38,681
- I'm sorry guys, but we caught one.
- Okay.

28
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:40,016
- Will you cash me out?
- Yeah.

29
00:01:40,808 --> 00:01:41,851
Have fun.

30
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:55,323
There you are.

31
00:01:55,448 --> 00:01:56,490
I should come by sometime

32
00:01:56,616 --> 00:01:58,117
when there's not a corpse here
to meet us.

33
00:01:59,327 --> 00:02:00,828
Are you playing poker again, Lisbon?

34
00:02:01,203 --> 00:02:02,288
It's getting the better of you.

35
00:02:02,955 --> 00:02:05,124
- Losing money?
- Losing skin's more like it.

36
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,584
Bertram's been in a bad mood.

37
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:09,795
Hi. I'm Agent Lisbon
with the CBI.

38
00:02:10,379 --> 00:02:11,756
Christos Papadakis.

39
00:02:12,381 --> 00:02:15,134
I am Dean of the university's
Natural Science Department

40
00:02:15,259 --> 00:02:18,220
so... the museum
is my responsibility.

41
00:02:18,471 --> 00:02:19,513
Please follow me.

42
00:02:21,182 --> 00:02:23,434
I believe your team is the one

43
00:02:23,559 --> 00:02:26,270
who investigated the death
of Professor Montero.

44
00:02:26,395 --> 00:02:27,438
Yeah, we were.

45
00:02:27,897 --> 00:02:29,398
We have a bit of a walk.

46
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:32,735
The museum labs are
in the east wing of the museum.

47
00:02:33,569 --> 00:02:35,154
What can you tell us
about the victim?

48
00:02:36,238 --> 00:02:39,325
- We think it is Linda Parfrey.
- You think it's her?

49
00:02:40,284 --> 00:02:42,703
Linda has been missing
for some time, and...

50
00:02:43,996 --> 00:02:45,539
Well, you'll see.

51
00:02:47,291 --> 00:02:51,212
Ah, this is the student who found Linda.
Tell...tell them your name.

52
00:02:51,754 --> 00:02:54,465
Uh, Greg.
Uh, Gregory Lewis.

53
00:02:54,674 --> 00:02:56,467
I found her around midnight.

54
00:02:56,926 --> 00:02:58,010
Tell me what happened.

55
00:02:58,844 --> 00:03:02,348
Well, by sheer luck, I-I happened upon
a very nice tree shrew.

56
00:03:02,556 --> 00:03:04,266
I've never examined one before

57
00:03:04,433 --> 00:03:06,519
and I-I brought it in to
strip the flesh off.

58
00:03:06,686 --> 00:03:08,479
You know,
to keep the skeleton.

59
00:03:08,854 --> 00:03:12,066
- How do you do that?
- Oh, we put 'em in these tanks.

60
00:03:13,984 --> 00:03:15,611
How does that strip them
of their flesh?

61
00:03:16,070 --> 00:03:18,948
- Flesh-eating bugs.
- Flesh-eating bugs. Right.

62
00:03:19,198 --> 00:03:20,241
Right.

63
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,618
That's when I found Linda.

64
00:03:32,878 --> 00:03:33,921
Okay.

65
00:03:46,890 --> 00:03:50,018
- Yeah, that's Linda's ring, all right.
- So it is Linda Parfrey.

66
00:03:50,352 --> 00:03:52,020
Why dump a body
into a beetle tank?

67
00:03:52,563 --> 00:03:54,273
The museum doesn't have
great security

68
00:03:54,398 --> 00:03:56,775
but it's not the kind of place
you can sneak a body out of.

69
00:03:57,151 --> 00:03:59,111
Unless you make it small enough
to fit in a duffel bag.

70
00:03:59,278 --> 00:04:00,738
So the killer was waiting
for the bugs to eat it

71
00:04:00,904 --> 00:04:02,656
down to the skeleton
so they could sneak it out?

72
00:04:03,157 --> 00:04:06,493
{\an8}Possibly. Uh, we found
a scientific tool in the tank.

73
00:04:06,785 --> 00:04:07,828
It could be the murder weapon.

74
00:04:08,245 --> 00:04:10,497
So the killer's probably
another scientist then.

75
00:04:10,914 --> 00:04:13,000
Or someone that works at the museum.

76
00:04:13,459 --> 00:04:14,585
{\an8}When was Linda last seen?

77
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,879
{\an8}Linda's been missing
a couple weeks.

78
00:04:17,129 --> 00:04:18,797
{\an8}People thought she was
out camping near Yosemite

79
00:04:18,922 --> 00:04:20,424
{\an8}doing some kind of research.

80
00:04:20,799 --> 00:04:22,259
{\an8}Then someone noticed
her car was on campus

81
00:04:22,426 --> 00:04:23,635
{\an8}and they realized
she was missing.

82
00:04:24,052 --> 00:04:26,930
{\an8}- "Who filed the missing persons report?"
- Dr. Sonia Kidd.

83
00:04:27,556 --> 00:04:30,184
{\an8}Head of the biology department
at the university and Linda's adviser.

84
00:04:30,851 --> 00:04:32,144
Her name sounds familiar.

85
00:04:32,603 --> 00:04:34,730
{\an8}Yeah, she's written a couple books.
Popular science stuff.

86
00:04:34,855 --> 00:04:36,857
{\an8}One's called
"Natural Born Killers:"

87
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:38,650
{\an8}"The Evolutionary Roots of Murder."

88
00:04:39,359 --> 00:04:41,028
{\an8}Cute. Jane and I will go talk to her.

89
00:04:41,278 --> 00:04:44,406
{\an8}And, uh, Linda had an office
here at the museum.

90
00:04:44,573 --> 00:04:46,408
- You and Cho check it out.
- You got it, boss.

91
00:04:56,043 --> 00:04:58,045
{\an8}We thought she was lost
in the mountains.

92
00:04:58,754 --> 00:05:00,088
{\an8}But she was here
the whole time.

93
00:05:01,298 --> 00:05:02,591
{\an8}It's too horrible for words.

94
00:05:02,966 --> 00:05:04,802
{\an8}What did she study?
What kind of animal?

95
00:05:05,219 --> 00:05:06,261
Why does that matter?

96
00:05:07,471 --> 00:05:10,057
{\an8}Nobody picks what they dedicate
their life to by accident.

97
00:05:10,265 --> 00:05:11,308
It says something.

98
00:05:11,683 --> 00:05:14,728
{\an8}I study primate brains.
What does that say about me?

99
00:05:15,062 --> 00:05:17,105
{\an8}You have dark thoughts
inside your head

100
00:05:17,231 --> 00:05:19,316
{\an8}that you want to believe
are normal and natural.

101
00:05:20,192 --> 00:05:21,360
Which they are, of course.

102
00:05:23,111 --> 00:05:24,404
{\an8}Linda was an ornithologist.

103
00:05:25,572 --> 00:05:28,200
{\an8}Her doctorate was on the diet
of the northern flicker.

104
00:05:29,409 --> 00:05:30,452
A kind of woodpecker.

105
00:05:31,912 --> 00:05:33,163
Interesting.

106
00:05:33,914 --> 00:05:34,957
{\an8}Tenacious.

107
00:05:36,166 --> 00:05:38,877
Bang their head against the problem
until they solve it.

108
00:05:39,711 --> 00:05:41,338
- Was that Linda?
- It was.

109
00:05:42,172 --> 00:05:44,174
{\an8}Although woodpeckers

110
00:05:44,341 --> 00:05:46,510
{\an8}don't bang their heads against things
so much as they use their bills

111
00:05:46,635 --> 00:05:47,678
to drill holes.

112
00:05:48,011 --> 00:05:51,223
{\an8}Yes. Well, the subconscious mind is...

113
00:05:52,182 --> 00:05:54,351
isn't really a stickler
about that kind of thing.

114
00:05:55,143 --> 00:05:56,478
{\an8}Was she a good student?

115
00:05:57,145 --> 00:05:58,188
{\an8}Brilliant.

116
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:02,067
{\an8}She had already co-authored several papers
in prestigious journals.

117
00:06:02,693 --> 00:06:05,195
{\an8}- Well, who was jealous?
- Nobody.

118
00:06:05,779 --> 00:06:08,490
{\an8}This is the top biology department
on the West Coast.

119
00:06:09,491 --> 00:06:10,742
{\an8}Best and brightest only.

120
00:06:11,827 --> 00:06:15,956
{\an8}The success of one of us helps all of us.
We're a family.

121
00:06:16,915 --> 00:06:20,460
{\an8}Well, people murder family members
every day. It's natural.

122
00:06:21,044 --> 00:06:22,421
Murder is natural.

123
00:06:23,171 --> 00:06:24,339
But abnormal.

124
00:06:24,715 --> 00:06:28,635
Teachers don't know there's scandals
unless of course they are the scandal.

125
00:06:31,889 --> 00:06:32,931
Are we done?

126
00:06:33,557 --> 00:06:35,767
Yeah. For now. We're gonna
need to talk to some students

127
00:06:35,893 --> 00:06:39,688
{\an8}and visit the gift shop
which is left out the door, isn't it?

128
00:06:40,647 --> 00:06:41,690
Yes.

129
00:06:44,443 --> 00:06:46,028
{\an8}Excuse me.
What are you doing?

130
00:06:46,528 --> 00:06:49,156
- Who are you?
- I'm Agent Rigsby. This is Agent Cho.

131
00:06:49,323 --> 00:06:52,576
- We're with CBI. And you are?
- Megan Parker.

132
00:06:52,951 --> 00:06:55,162
I was Linda's classmate
in the doctoral program.

133
00:06:55,704 --> 00:06:58,165
- And I have the office next door.
- What are you doing here?

134
00:06:58,707 --> 00:07:01,460
I just came to get my copy of
"Understanding Macro-Evolution"

135
00:07:01,585 --> 00:07:03,253
before you guys
sealed the place off.

136
00:07:05,213 --> 00:07:07,341
- Did you know Linda well?
- She was rock star.

137
00:07:07,883 --> 00:07:10,677
A rock star who could use
a makeover, but still.

138
00:07:11,053 --> 00:07:12,679
She got the Dean's fellowship
this year.

139
00:07:12,971 --> 00:07:14,097
That's like 100 grand.

140
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:16,642
The rest of us will be
paying off our student loans

141
00:07:16,767 --> 00:07:18,185
with our social security checks.

142
00:07:18,602 --> 00:07:19,895
What's with all the taxidermy?

143
00:07:20,145 --> 00:07:21,563
I thought Linda just studied
woodpeckers.

144
00:07:22,189 --> 00:07:23,649
No, Linda managed
the specimen rooms

145
00:07:23,857 --> 00:07:26,026
for her work-study,
so she got the overflow.

146
00:07:27,444 --> 00:07:28,487
Drugs?

147
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:35,702
- Sugar.
- She didn't do drugs.

148
00:07:36,203 --> 00:07:37,412
Not even diet soda.

149
00:07:38,121 --> 00:07:39,164
What about
her personal life?

150
00:07:39,289 --> 00:07:40,666
- What about it?
- Did she have one?

151
00:07:41,375 --> 00:07:43,126
We didn't talk about
that stuff very much.

152
00:07:43,752 --> 00:07:45,921
I know she had a boyfriend
outside the school.

153
00:07:46,463 --> 00:07:48,090
But that's all I know.

154
00:07:50,842 --> 00:07:52,970
Hey, Jane, call me when you get this.

155
00:07:53,136 --> 00:07:54,972
I think I found
Linda's classmates.

156
00:07:55,097 --> 00:07:56,139
Look for the elephants.

157
00:07:56,264 --> 00:07:58,892
Most species stay in stasis
for long periods of time.

158
00:07:59,226 --> 00:08:02,187
- Sorry, can I help you?
- I'm Agent Lisbon with the CBI.

159
00:08:02,396 --> 00:08:04,564
Professor Friedman?
Adjunct Professor.

160
00:08:04,898 --> 00:08:06,650
Yes, adjunct Professor
Paul Friedman

161
00:08:06,775 --> 00:08:09,069
which, as you can see, engenders
a great deal of respect

162
00:08:09,319 --> 00:08:10,362
from the students.

163
00:08:10,487 --> 00:08:12,447
Uh, I teach a couple seminars
under Dr. Kidd.

164
00:08:12,698 --> 00:08:13,991
I'm sorry to interrupt

165
00:08:14,157 --> 00:08:16,076
but I'm looking for information
on Linda Parfrey.

166
00:08:16,410 --> 00:08:19,287
- These are her classmates, right?
- Yeah. We all know Linda.

167
00:08:20,038 --> 00:08:22,874
Do you know anything that might be
helpful with the investigation?

168
00:08:26,628 --> 00:08:30,173
We know that Linda had a boyfriend.
We'd like to talk to him.

169
00:08:30,382 --> 00:08:32,300
Ex-boyfriend, I guess.

170
00:08:33,385 --> 00:08:35,637
She didn't talk much about it,
but late one night in the lab

171
00:08:35,846 --> 00:08:36,888
she told me they broke up.

172
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:40,183
- When was that?
- About a week before she went missing.

173
00:08:40,684 --> 00:08:43,270
You don't think he did it, do you?
I mean, he's a cop.

174
00:08:43,562 --> 00:08:46,064
- A cop? Do you know his name?
- Yeah.

175
00:08:46,773 --> 00:08:49,901
Officer Ray Moran, San Francisco P.D.
Beat cop.

176
00:08:50,152 --> 00:08:51,403
All right, talk to his captain.

177
00:08:51,570 --> 00:08:53,572
See if we can bring Moran in
for an interview.

178
00:08:53,822 --> 00:08:56,491
Put Cho on it.
Jane, too, if he ever comes in.

179
00:08:56,950 --> 00:08:57,993
You got it.

180
00:08:58,118 --> 00:08:59,161
Hey, boss?

181
00:08:59,411 --> 00:09:01,288
I did it. I made it
into the program.

182
00:09:01,913 --> 00:09:04,041
White Hat? You got in?
That's great.

183
00:09:04,374 --> 00:09:06,001
Yeah. A month in L.A.

184
00:09:06,668 --> 00:09:09,212
I'll be staring at a computer screen
the whole time but still.

185
00:09:09,588 --> 00:09:11,048
The team's gonna miss you.

186
00:09:11,298 --> 00:09:14,092
You said you'd talk to Bertram
about the stipend request.

187
00:09:14,384 --> 00:09:15,886
The deposit's due in two days.

188
00:09:16,386 --> 00:09:18,972
- I'll swing by on my way back.
- Thanks, boss.

189
00:09:21,183 --> 00:09:22,267
"It's called White Hat."

190
00:09:22,392 --> 00:09:24,728
It's an advanced computer
investigation training program

191
00:09:24,895 --> 00:09:26,188
taught by actual hackers.

192
00:09:26,438 --> 00:09:29,399
It's so advanced, I don't know what
she's saying when she talks about it.

193
00:09:31,777 --> 00:09:36,031
I'd like to rush a request for a training
stipend to cover the costs.

194
00:09:36,156 --> 00:09:37,491
You think Manchester's
been cheating?

195
00:09:39,367 --> 00:09:40,410
- What?
- At poker.

196
00:09:40,535 --> 00:09:41,578
No.

197
00:09:41,703 --> 00:09:45,916
Well, he's just so damn smug.
You know, "Goodnight Moon," he says...

198
00:09:46,083 --> 00:09:48,418
- what the hell is that?
- It's just a game.

199
00:09:48,752 --> 00:09:49,836
You don't believe that.

200
00:09:52,172 --> 00:09:53,215
Sir...

201
00:09:53,924 --> 00:09:58,512
- No...oh, what was that about Van Pelt?
- Her training stipend.

202
00:09:59,930 --> 00:10:00,972
Times are tight.

203
00:10:01,139 --> 00:10:03,600
We've cut all outside
training funds from the budget.

204
00:10:03,809 --> 00:10:04,851
No, request denied.

205
00:10:12,317 --> 00:10:13,360
Hey.

206
00:10:14,736 --> 00:10:18,031
- Lisbon said she lost you.
- Well, I come bearing gifts.

207
00:10:19,407 --> 00:10:20,450
Grace.

208
00:10:21,618 --> 00:10:25,580
Jelly dinosaurs are for me...
for later.

209
00:10:26,623 --> 00:10:28,083
And...

210
00:10:28,583 --> 00:10:31,419
- I'm guessing you're a Triceratops man.
- Yeah.

211
00:10:31,795 --> 00:10:33,630
Rigsby is a T. Rex.

212
00:10:34,923 --> 00:10:35,966
Any news on the case?

213
00:10:36,091 --> 00:10:38,426
Yeah, the victim's ex-boyfriend
is an S.F.P.D. beat cop.

214
00:10:38,593 --> 00:10:40,470
He's on patrol right now.
I was just gonna go talk to him.

215
00:10:40,595 --> 00:10:42,389
- On the streets?
- Yeah.

216
00:10:42,681 --> 00:10:44,599
Let's go. I haven't been
on the streets for a while.

217
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:46,143
Let's do it.

218
00:10:47,894 --> 00:10:49,729
- Come on.
- Thanks?

219
00:10:50,272 --> 00:10:54,568
I don't know. He was like, uh, 5'6".
Not fat, really. Just a big face.

220
00:10:54,901 --> 00:10:55,944
Brown hair.

221
00:10:56,069 --> 00:10:58,113
He didn't even have a gun.
Why'd you give him the money?

222
00:10:58,446 --> 00:11:00,407
I didn't give it to him,
he took it!

223
00:11:00,907 --> 00:11:02,325
What was he dressed like,
this guy?

224
00:11:02,701 --> 00:11:03,743
You guys from CBI?

225
00:11:04,536 --> 00:11:06,121
Agent Cho.
This is Patrick Jane.

226
00:11:06,496 --> 00:11:08,915
Uh, look, I want to talk to you guys
but we gotta handle this, okay?

227
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:10,292
Let me catch up with you
in a couple hours.

228
00:11:10,834 --> 00:11:12,669
- A couple hours?
- Yeah, we just got this.

229
00:11:12,836 --> 00:11:16,047
I mean, we gotta put out a crime broadcast.
We gotta cruise the neighborhood.

230
00:11:16,423 --> 00:11:17,549
Then there's the paperwork.

231
00:11:17,966 --> 00:11:20,260
Well, can't help you
with the paperwork.

232
00:11:20,427 --> 00:11:22,137
- He had a hood. He came in...
- Why'd you give him the money?

233
00:11:22,345 --> 00:11:23,930
Sir, we're asking
the questions here.

234
00:11:24,389 --> 00:11:25,599
Just tell me what happened.

235
00:11:26,224 --> 00:11:28,643
So this dude comes up
while I'm cashing out the register

236
00:11:29,019 --> 00:11:30,312
asks for a calling card.

237
00:11:31,021 --> 00:11:32,480
I just turned for one second

238
00:11:32,731 --> 00:11:34,941
and he reached through the gap
and grabbed a stack of $20s.

239
00:11:35,108 --> 00:11:39,362
You wait until you're finished cashing out
before you do another transaction!

240
00:11:39,529 --> 00:11:42,407
Look, the guy had money.
He didn't look shady or anything.

241
00:11:42,532 --> 00:11:45,118
- How was I supposed to know?
- Hey. Excuse me.

242
00:11:45,243 --> 00:11:46,870
Show me how he reached
through the gap again.

243
00:11:53,418 --> 00:11:54,794
All right.
Pull your hand out.

244
00:11:56,254 --> 00:11:57,297
Huh?

245
00:11:57,422 --> 00:11:59,257
That's how they catch a raccoon,
you know that?

246
00:11:59,883 --> 00:12:02,010
So the guy couldn't have grabbed
the cash through the gap.

247
00:12:02,385 --> 00:12:04,387
There wasn't another guy,
was there, buddy?

248
00:12:04,763 --> 00:12:07,599
- You! You're the thief!
- I'm the thief?

249
00:12:07,724 --> 00:12:09,017
You're the thief.

250
00:12:09,601 --> 00:12:11,645
With what you're paying me,
I'm the thief?

251
00:12:12,145 --> 00:12:13,188
Work's done here, buddy.

252
00:12:13,313 --> 00:12:14,856
I think you can get your man
to do the paperwork.

253
00:12:16,191 --> 00:12:17,234
Look...

254
00:12:18,026 --> 00:12:19,069
no, no, no.

255
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:23,847
When did you
and Linda meet?

256
00:12:24,347 --> 00:12:27,517
Last year. I was moonlighting
doing security at the museum.

257
00:12:27,684 --> 00:12:28,893
And how was the relationship?

258
00:12:30,353 --> 00:12:32,564
Her life was more together
than anybody I ever met.

259
00:12:33,064 --> 00:12:34,107
She was so focused.

260
00:12:34,983 --> 00:12:36,025
Got to me, too, I guess.

261
00:12:36,151 --> 00:12:37,777
I even started studying
for the sergeant's exam.

262
00:12:38,194 --> 00:12:39,237
And then you dumped her?

263
00:12:39,571 --> 00:12:41,114
- You think I was happy about it?
- You tell me.

264
00:12:41,614 --> 00:12:43,616
About two months ago
I got an anonymous e-mail

265
00:12:43,992 --> 00:12:45,118
with a link in it

266
00:12:45,493 --> 00:12:46,953
to her online dating profile.

267
00:12:48,329 --> 00:12:49,372
I couldn't believe it.

268
00:12:49,873 --> 00:12:51,249
And she'd started
seeing other people?

269
00:12:52,125 --> 00:12:53,710
I... I don't know
if she started.

270
00:12:54,127 --> 00:12:55,170
She was just shopping around.

271
00:12:55,295 --> 00:12:56,337
Maybe she'd been doing it
the whole time.

272
00:12:56,462 --> 00:12:57,964
- You talk to her about it?
- Yeah.

273
00:12:59,465 --> 00:13:00,508
Big fight.

274
00:13:01,759 --> 00:13:02,802
You get rough with her?

275
00:13:04,512 --> 00:13:05,597
I don't have that in me.

276
00:13:06,347 --> 00:13:08,433
Hurt a woman?
Especially not Linda.

277
00:13:08,641 --> 00:13:09,726
She have an explanation?

278
00:13:11,227 --> 00:13:13,605
She denied it, you know,
but what else was she gonna do?

279
00:13:14,272 --> 00:13:16,024
I can't take cheating.
I had to let her go.

280
00:13:16,649 --> 00:13:18,193
- Did you know who sent you the profile?
- No.

281
00:13:18,651 --> 00:13:19,819
And you weren't curious?

282
00:13:20,695 --> 00:13:21,946
I don't know. I...

283
00:13:22,197 --> 00:13:23,781
Some guy's girlfriend's
in the same boat as me

284
00:13:23,948 --> 00:13:25,700
somebody's holding a grudge.

285
00:13:26,075 --> 00:13:28,161
Only thing that mattered
was I wasn't enough for her.

286
00:13:28,703 --> 00:13:30,788
And after you broke up,
you never spoke to her again?

287
00:13:30,914 --> 00:13:31,956
No.

288
00:13:34,667 --> 00:13:35,835
I should have known.

289
00:13:36,502 --> 00:13:39,589
You know, she had smarts.
She was gonna have a big life.

290
00:13:41,382 --> 00:13:43,343
What'd she want
with a mook like me, right?

291
00:13:47,764 --> 00:13:48,806
Do you have something?

292
00:13:49,015 --> 00:13:50,934
Here's the dating profile
somebody sent Moran.

293
00:13:52,101 --> 00:13:55,688
Get a hold of the dating site.
See if anybody contacted her

294
00:13:55,855 --> 00:13:57,023
around the time
she went missing.

295
00:13:57,190 --> 00:13:59,609
- I don't think Linda went on any dates.
- Well, why not?

296
00:14:00,193 --> 00:14:01,903
The address that sent Moran the e-mail

297
00:14:02,070 --> 00:14:04,197
was used to set up an account
with the dating site.

298
00:14:05,406 --> 00:14:08,826
- Did you find them?
- Sort of. I logged into the account.

299
00:14:09,494 --> 00:14:10,912
Don't you need a password for that?

300
00:14:11,079 --> 00:14:13,665
I spent two minutes using
the most common passwords.

301
00:14:13,790 --> 00:14:15,500
- It was "qwerty."
- "Qwerty"?

302
00:14:16,042 --> 00:14:17,877
Q-w-e-r-t-y.

303
00:14:18,044 --> 00:14:20,004
It's the fifth most
common password.

304
00:14:20,421 --> 00:14:23,091
- Guess whose profile it was.
- Linda.

305
00:14:23,633 --> 00:14:26,719
So whoever sent Moran the profile
also created it.

306
00:14:26,928 --> 00:14:28,221
Yep. Profile's a fake.

307
00:14:28,763 --> 00:14:31,766
Someone was trying to break up
Linda and Officer Moran.

308
00:14:31,975 --> 00:14:33,351
Nice work.

309
00:14:34,018 --> 00:14:37,021
Look, I know you're disappointed
about the training program.

310
00:14:37,814 --> 00:14:39,899
- We'll get you in next year.
- It's fine.

311
00:14:44,862 --> 00:14:47,490
Bertram's supposed to be
this real smooth player

312
00:14:47,615 --> 00:14:50,952
but he loses a couple of hands of poker
and he has to bum everybody else out.

313
00:14:51,119 --> 00:14:54,038
Linda wanted
"a man with a super hot body."

314
00:14:54,789 --> 00:14:57,709
- The fake dating profile, right?
- You were saying something?

315
00:14:57,875 --> 00:14:59,794
Bertram hanging Van Pelt out to dry.

316
00:15:00,003 --> 00:15:03,423
He's just mad because
he's losing big money at poker.

317
00:15:03,881 --> 00:15:06,301
Well, some people like to push
their pain onto other people

318
00:15:06,718 --> 00:15:08,594
weaker people, and there's
always someone weaker.

319
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:10,930
- That's just the circle of life.
- Cheery.

320
00:15:11,764 --> 00:15:14,058
Bertram doesn't want to win,
Lisbon, he has to.

321
00:15:14,183 --> 00:15:16,060
It's why he is where he is.

322
00:15:16,436 --> 00:15:18,730
The idea of losing
is just intolerable to him.

323
00:15:19,981 --> 00:15:22,358
Why would somebody want to break
Linda and her boyfriend up?

324
00:15:22,734 --> 00:15:25,320
- Romantic rivalry?
- Yeah, possibly.

325
00:15:25,862 --> 00:15:29,324
"I'm looking for brains,
brawn, the total package."

326
00:15:29,532 --> 00:15:32,035
Van Pelt's trying to trace the e-mail
but she hit a dead end.

327
00:15:32,201 --> 00:15:35,330
Well, whoever authored this
left their fingerprints all over it.

328
00:15:35,705 --> 00:15:37,081
"No half-steppers."

329
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:39,334
"A genuine badass."

330
00:15:39,584 --> 00:15:41,044
"Feather my nest."

331
00:15:41,419 --> 00:15:43,379
- Gather the troops, Lisbon.
- Why?

332
00:15:44,630 --> 00:15:46,174
It's time to play bingo.

333
00:15:48,426 --> 00:15:51,888
So we have a list of words
from the fake dating profile.

334
00:15:52,221 --> 00:15:53,264
We talk to the students

335
00:15:53,473 --> 00:15:55,224
and compare what they say
to the words in the list

336
00:15:55,350 --> 00:15:57,268
and if what they say matches up
with the words on the list

337
00:15:57,393 --> 00:15:59,520
then they're the one
that wrote the profile?

338
00:15:59,645 --> 00:16:00,688
So far, so good.

339
00:16:00,897 --> 00:16:02,482
But how do we get them
to say the right words?

340
00:16:02,607 --> 00:16:03,649
What do we ask them about?

341
00:16:03,775 --> 00:16:05,068
You have to try to get
their words flowing.

342
00:16:05,234 --> 00:16:07,445
- Talk to them about their passions.
- Their passions?

343
00:16:07,695 --> 00:16:09,113
Animals, of course.

344
00:16:09,614 --> 00:16:11,491
Bugs. Critters.

345
00:16:12,617 --> 00:16:17,830
People use "coldblooded" to mean evil
as if a specie's thermo-physiology

346
00:16:17,955 --> 00:16:20,958
could determine its moral attributes.
That's ridiculous.

347
00:16:21,542 --> 00:16:23,544
Reptiles never invented
the atomic bomb.

348
00:16:24,921 --> 00:16:28,216
If you ask me, warm blood
is the problem.

349
00:16:29,133 --> 00:16:30,176
Know what I mean?

350
00:16:36,349 --> 00:16:37,392
Excuse me.

351
00:16:37,642 --> 00:16:41,312
So... skeletons.
Why are you into them?

352
00:16:41,437 --> 00:16:42,480
Why do I need a reason?

353
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:45,066
I'll just go with "weird."

354
00:16:45,608 --> 00:16:48,611
It's, like, under all this viscera
we wear

355
00:16:48,736 --> 00:16:50,196
there's...well,
there's something strong

356
00:16:50,321 --> 00:16:53,658
and...and clean and...and beautiful
that...that holds us together.

357
00:16:54,117 --> 00:16:56,494
You can't lose it.
I think...I think that's cool.

358
00:17:00,623 --> 00:17:01,666
Did I pass?

359
00:17:02,125 --> 00:17:03,876
I've taken a psych test before.

360
00:17:14,762 --> 00:17:15,805
Mr. Jane.

361
00:17:16,139 --> 00:17:17,557
We were just discussing you.

362
00:17:18,641 --> 00:17:21,102
Dean Papadakis told me
about your impressive memory.

363
00:17:21,894 --> 00:17:23,896
My own memory isn't as good
as it used to be.

364
00:17:24,355 --> 00:17:25,398
What's your secret?

365
00:17:26,941 --> 00:17:28,693
It's easy to remember
when you never forget.

366
00:17:30,695 --> 00:17:33,406
- What you doing?
- Laying bait for moths.

367
00:17:34,490 --> 00:17:37,952
They like sweetness
so I use brown bananas and beer.

368
00:17:38,786 --> 00:17:40,288
Delicious.

369
00:17:41,831 --> 00:17:43,958
Paul here discovered
a new species of moth.

370
00:17:44,500 --> 00:17:45,668
The California Jade moth.

371
00:17:46,002 --> 00:17:47,795
It's on display in our museum.

372
00:17:48,296 --> 00:17:49,547
It's rather a big deal.

373
00:17:49,797 --> 00:17:53,176
Yeah. Now there are 1,500,001
types of insects in the world.

374
00:17:53,801 --> 00:17:55,178
Well, that's false modesty.

375
00:17:55,428 --> 00:17:57,138
I guess so. It is a big deal.

376
00:17:57,597 --> 00:17:58,639
To moth people.

377
00:17:58,973 --> 00:18:00,057
To all of us.

378
00:18:01,434 --> 00:18:03,352
Congratulations
on your new addition.

379
00:18:03,728 --> 00:18:05,188
- Thank you.
- It's most impressive.

380
00:18:06,439 --> 00:18:07,482
Thank you.

381
00:18:12,487 --> 00:18:13,738
Uh, let me guess.

382
00:18:14,071 --> 00:18:16,908
You're studying buzzards and
you're laying a trap for one right now.

383
00:18:17,366 --> 00:18:18,618
As if.

384
00:18:18,993 --> 00:18:21,412
Mating habits
of the zalophus californianus.

385
00:18:21,913 --> 00:18:22,955
Sea lions.

386
00:18:23,122 --> 00:18:26,542
One badass male maintaining
a stable of females through combat.

387
00:18:28,002 --> 00:18:30,296
Huh. And that appeals to you?
A man fighting for you?

388
00:18:31,214 --> 00:18:34,383
Who doesn't want an Alpha male?
A guy who will fight for what's his?

389
00:18:34,675 --> 00:18:36,302
Yeah. Super hot.

390
00:18:37,136 --> 00:18:38,179
Sounds exciting.

391
00:18:38,554 --> 00:18:40,598
It's so cool.
While Paul chases moths

392
00:18:40,806 --> 00:18:43,059
and Jeanette messes around
with her lizards

393
00:18:43,684 --> 00:18:45,811
I head out for the islands
to watch the seals play.

394
00:18:46,812 --> 00:18:48,314
I'm not just a brain, see?

395
00:18:48,606 --> 00:18:49,857
I like to play, too.

396
00:18:50,358 --> 00:18:51,484
I'm the total package.

397
00:18:52,485 --> 00:18:56,280
"Badass", "super-hot",
"total package."

398
00:18:58,115 --> 00:18:59,158
Bingo!

399
00:19:00,284 --> 00:19:01,953
Whoo! Bingo!

400
00:19:07,681 --> 00:19:08,932
You wrote the fake
dating profile

401
00:19:09,099 --> 00:19:10,601
that made Linda and her
boyfriend break up.

402
00:19:11,644 --> 00:19:13,479
No law against it, is there?

403
00:19:13,729 --> 00:19:14,813
Maybe she found out it was you

404
00:19:14,980 --> 00:19:16,815
confronted you in the lab,
and things got out of hand.

405
00:19:17,149 --> 00:19:19,860
Look... hello!
I didn't kill Linda.

406
00:19:20,402 --> 00:19:21,695
So why did you want
to break them up?

407
00:19:24,657 --> 00:19:26,575
You don't even know
what it's like in the program.

408
00:19:27,493 --> 00:19:30,371
Dr. Kidd likes us to play
like we're one happy family.

409
00:19:30,913 --> 00:19:32,998
We're like a pack of hyenas
tearing each other apart.

410
00:19:33,457 --> 00:19:36,627
- Why?
- Money. Why else?

411
00:19:36,877 --> 00:19:39,004
Look, the museum doesn't get
all that stuff for free.

412
00:19:39,546 --> 00:19:42,132
It needs discoveries,
publicity, donations.

413
00:19:43,509 --> 00:19:45,469
Dr. Kidd wants our program
to be the best.

414
00:19:45,844 --> 00:19:48,263
So if you aren't producing,
so long.

415
00:19:48,597 --> 00:19:49,640
What does that mean?

416
00:19:50,516 --> 00:19:52,476
You ever heard of
survival of the fittest?

417
00:19:52,643 --> 00:19:54,269
'Cause Dr. Kidd sure has.

418
00:19:54,812 --> 00:19:56,689
She likes to weed out the ones
who can't contribute

419
00:19:56,814 --> 00:19:57,940
who can't hack it.

420
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:00,442
But nobody hacks it.
Not really.

421
00:20:00,651 --> 00:20:01,694
What do you mean?

422
00:20:02,236 --> 00:20:03,987
Jeanette? Lizard girl?

423
00:20:04,488 --> 00:20:07,157
She has a special place on the
back stairs where she goes to cry.

424
00:20:07,658 --> 00:20:11,453
Greg had a major breakdown last year
like throwing rat skulls out the window.

425
00:20:12,037 --> 00:20:15,666
And Paul? He was convinced
Dr. Kidd was gonna fire him

426
00:20:15,791 --> 00:20:17,418
until he discovered
that dumb moth.

427
00:20:17,584 --> 00:20:19,461
How about Linda?
How did she handle it?

428
00:20:21,130 --> 00:20:22,381
She was a machine.

429
00:20:23,716 --> 00:20:24,800
You're losing your mind

430
00:20:25,342 --> 00:20:28,595
and there she is working just as hard,
not even breaking a sweat.

431
00:20:29,805 --> 00:20:32,182
My mom wanted to kill me
for not getting the Dean's fellowship.

432
00:20:33,058 --> 00:20:35,477
And then Linda lands
that hot cop boyfriend?

433
00:20:36,061 --> 00:20:37,104
Her?

434
00:20:37,479 --> 00:20:38,522
I mean...

435
00:20:39,732 --> 00:20:40,774
Right?

436
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:44,737
So you made up the fake dating profile
to break them up.

437
00:20:47,322 --> 00:20:49,324
I just wanted to see
if I could hurt her.

438
00:20:50,659 --> 00:20:52,911
- If she could even be hurt.
- Did she know it was you?

439
00:20:54,538 --> 00:20:55,581
It wrecked her.

440
00:20:56,123 --> 00:20:59,001
The next day she messed up something stupid
and Dr. Kidd yelled at her.

441
00:21:00,586 --> 00:21:01,628
You know what?

442
00:21:02,004 --> 00:21:03,547
It felt really, really good.

443
00:21:03,964 --> 00:21:06,133
And I know that doesn't make me sound
like a nice person.

444
00:21:06,258 --> 00:21:08,010
No. No, it doesn't.

445
00:21:11,805 --> 00:21:13,640
It's like "Lord of the Flies" over there.

446
00:21:13,766 --> 00:21:16,143
I didn't know the world of science
was so cutthroat.

447
00:21:16,435 --> 00:21:17,478
Well, like the man said

448
00:21:17,603 --> 00:21:20,481
academic politics can be vicious,
because the stakes are small.

449
00:21:20,981 --> 00:21:22,024
Very small, indeed.

450
00:21:22,149 --> 00:21:23,692
Tomorrow morning we need
to go back to the museum.

451
00:21:23,901 --> 00:21:25,819
- Why?
- Education.

452
00:21:26,570 --> 00:21:28,447
But now I have to do something.

453
00:21:30,866 --> 00:21:33,619
And the A.G. knows damn well
the indictment won't stick.

454
00:21:34,828 --> 00:21:35,871
I don't have time for this.

455
00:21:37,039 --> 00:21:39,291
Nobody rides for free.
I mean, he knows that.

456
00:21:41,335 --> 00:21:42,961
What... what, you came here
to play games?

457
00:21:44,421 --> 00:21:46,131
I heard you've been losing

458
00:21:46,256 --> 00:21:48,175
and, uh, it's been
affecting your work.

459
00:21:50,969 --> 00:21:52,387
Uh, I'll call you back.

460
00:21:53,847 --> 00:21:55,808
Look, the cards haven't been
falling my way.

461
00:21:56,475 --> 00:21:57,518
Oh, come on.

462
00:21:59,228 --> 00:22:00,270
You believe in luck?

463
00:22:01,814 --> 00:22:02,856
No.

464
00:22:04,024 --> 00:22:05,526
Lisbon says you're
pretty good.

465
00:22:08,320 --> 00:22:10,072
Margaret, cancel my 6:00.

466
00:22:17,371 --> 00:22:18,580
Two pair.

467
00:22:19,498 --> 00:22:20,666
Four of a kind.

468
00:22:20,916 --> 00:22:21,959
See?

469
00:22:22,584 --> 00:22:24,503
- I know how to play poker.
- Of course you do.

470
00:22:24,628 --> 00:22:26,797
You play the man,
not the cards.

471
00:22:27,047 --> 00:22:28,882
Right? I know the odds,
I know myself.

472
00:22:29,007 --> 00:22:30,926
You know people,
you know how to control them.

473
00:22:31,301 --> 00:22:33,053
Not that you always care.

474
00:22:33,387 --> 00:22:34,805
Uh... what's that mean?

475
00:22:34,930 --> 00:22:37,099
Well, you win more flies
with honey than with vinegar

476
00:22:37,224 --> 00:22:39,059
but you use vinegar anyway.

477
00:22:39,393 --> 00:22:40,435
Is this about Lisbon?

478
00:22:40,561 --> 00:22:42,396
You're very fond of
the fake tell, aren't you?

479
00:22:42,980 --> 00:22:46,567
Before you bet, you look away
and you breathe out very slowly

480
00:22:46,692 --> 00:22:49,194
like you're trying to...
calm yourself.

481
00:22:52,114 --> 00:22:53,156
Yes.

482
00:22:55,158 --> 00:22:57,619
Well, you have a tell
inside the fake tell.

483
00:22:57,828 --> 00:22:59,037
When you're bluffing

484
00:22:59,997 --> 00:23:02,541
the breathing is audible.

485
00:23:03,125 --> 00:23:05,043
The hell it is.
I control myself.

486
00:23:05,252 --> 00:23:07,254
You get yourself a good hand
and puff away like that

487
00:23:07,379 --> 00:23:09,339
Manchester will for sure
think you're bluffing.

488
00:23:10,674 --> 00:23:11,717
Trust me.

489
00:23:27,024 --> 00:23:29,234
Mr. Jane.
Can I help you?

490
00:23:29,776 --> 00:23:32,487
I hope so. Uh, you still want
to learn my memory trick?

491
00:23:33,739 --> 00:23:34,781
I would.

492
00:23:34,948 --> 00:23:36,992
Yeah, you teach a master class

493
00:23:37,117 --> 00:23:39,703
with all of Linda's
fellow classmates, huh?

494
00:23:40,162 --> 00:23:41,955
Paul and I will be teaching it
this afternoon.

495
00:23:42,539 --> 00:23:43,582
Yeah.

496
00:23:43,832 --> 00:23:46,835
Well, how about I put on a memory seminar
for you and your class?

497
00:23:47,669 --> 00:23:48,712
That would be terrific.

498
00:23:50,339 --> 00:23:51,465
But don't you have
a murder to solve?

499
00:23:51,673 --> 00:23:52,758
Eh, all in good time.

500
00:23:52,925 --> 00:23:54,259
I'm gonna need some tables

501
00:23:54,384 --> 00:23:56,803
possibly three or four
across the back there.

502
00:23:56,929 --> 00:23:57,971
Okay.

503
00:23:58,096 --> 00:23:59,139
And, Linda's office

504
00:23:59,264 --> 00:24:01,642
- it's full of animals... dead ones.
- It is.

505
00:24:01,975 --> 00:24:04,186
We'll need access to that,
for props.

506
00:24:04,561 --> 00:24:06,647
Okay. I assume...

507
00:24:07,064 --> 00:24:08,732
That you have some ulterior motive
for all of this.

508
00:24:08,899 --> 00:24:10,359
Do you have a suspect?

509
00:24:11,777 --> 00:24:13,654
There's one thing I'd like you
to confirm for me.

510
00:24:14,363 --> 00:24:15,405
What is that?

511
00:24:15,530 --> 00:24:18,700
Just what is the diet of the northern
flicker woodpecker?

512
00:24:23,830 --> 00:24:25,082
Yeah. Sure.

513
00:24:25,999 --> 00:24:27,417
How can you like Triceratops
more than...

514
00:24:27,626 --> 00:24:28,669
T. Rex?

515
00:24:28,835 --> 00:24:29,878
T. Rex are losers.

516
00:24:30,587 --> 00:24:32,714
Little arms. It's a big
chicken with teeth.

517
00:24:33,256 --> 00:24:35,550
Yeah, and Triceratops
is basically a pig with horns.

518
00:24:35,801 --> 00:24:37,010
And a shield on its head.

519
00:24:37,844 --> 00:24:39,221
Beats a giant chicken
any day of the week.

520
00:24:39,346 --> 00:24:41,640
In your dreams.
What did Jane want?

521
00:24:42,849 --> 00:24:44,851
- He wants us back at the museum.
- What are we gonna do?

522
00:24:45,978 --> 00:24:47,854
- Museum heist.
- Why?

523
00:24:48,397 --> 00:24:50,107
I stopped asking questions
a long time ago. Come on.

524
00:24:56,279 --> 00:24:57,322
What's this all about?

525
00:24:57,447 --> 00:24:59,282
We just need to cover
our bases, sir.

526
00:24:59,408 --> 00:25:00,867
What does this have to do
with the investigation?

527
00:25:00,993 --> 00:25:03,203
I can't discuss ongoing details
of an investigation.

528
00:25:03,328 --> 00:25:06,456
I'm gonna need to look
at one of these, uh, spiders.

529
00:25:06,748 --> 00:25:08,000
The tarantulas?

530
00:25:08,417 --> 00:25:11,378
Hey, kids, come check this out
spiders.

531
00:25:11,545 --> 00:25:13,588
Uh yes, the tarantulas.

532
00:25:13,755 --> 00:25:15,757
I don't see why it is necessary.

533
00:25:15,882 --> 00:25:18,885
Don't worry, sir. This is,
official police business.

534
00:25:19,011 --> 00:25:21,888
- Detective please.
- I assure you, it won't take too long.

535
00:25:22,014 --> 00:25:23,598
Wait, what are you doing?

536
00:25:24,057 --> 00:25:26,435
I'm gonna need to take
a close look at this...

537
00:25:26,727 --> 00:25:27,978
- Spider here.
- No.

538
00:25:28,854 --> 00:25:32,941
- This one specifically here, this spider.
- No, no, what are you doing?

539
00:25:33,775 --> 00:25:35,569
- Don't pick it up.
- Here we go.

540
00:25:35,736 --> 00:25:38,572
Just... no. Oh, oh, please.
Be very careful.

541
00:25:39,197 --> 00:25:41,033
There we go.
Uh, yeah.

542
00:25:41,324 --> 00:25:46,538
I don't see why this is necessary.
Don't worry children, it's...

543
00:25:46,830 --> 00:25:49,624
- There we go.
- Wait, what are you doing?

544
00:25:49,958 --> 00:25:52,252
Yeah, we're gonna have to, uh...

545
00:25:52,377 --> 00:25:54,588
- Dust this for prints.
- Do what?

546
00:25:54,921 --> 00:25:56,673
Pretty colors, ha kids?

547
00:25:57,007 --> 00:25:58,091
Ow!

548
00:25:58,300 --> 00:26:01,261
- Ow! Oh, ow! It bit me.
- Oh no! It bit him!

549
00:26:01,386 --> 00:26:02,804
- Wait what?
- It bit me.

550
00:26:05,682 --> 00:26:08,769
Let me call someone.
This seems dangerous.

551
00:26:10,353 --> 00:26:11,688
- Hey...
- Ow! Ow! Look.

552
00:26:11,813 --> 00:26:13,231
See? It's deadly.

553
00:26:13,440 --> 00:26:14,566
It feels a little strange.

554
00:26:14,775 --> 00:26:16,818
- I don't see anything.
- Oh, I'm feeling woozy.

555
00:26:17,069 --> 00:26:18,153
No, I'm just kidding.
I'm fine.

556
00:26:18,779 --> 00:26:20,989
Don't play with spiders, kids.
Okay?

557
00:26:27,477 --> 00:26:29,145
We've got a real treat today.

558
00:26:29,479 --> 00:26:31,731
You could call it
"evolution in action."

559
00:26:32,399 --> 00:26:35,110
Mr. Patrick Jane
is coming to give us a seminar

560
00:26:35,318 --> 00:26:36,736
on the upper limits of memory.

561
00:26:37,529 --> 00:26:39,656
- Are we in the classroom?
- Yes, we are.

562
00:26:40,073 --> 00:26:41,116
Great.

563
00:26:43,076 --> 00:26:47,205
The human memory is far more capable
than most people realize.

564
00:26:47,330 --> 00:26:49,374
I've been in this room once before.

565
00:26:49,666 --> 00:26:51,001
But when I was in here...

566
00:26:53,962 --> 00:26:55,005
I paid attention.

567
00:26:56,256 --> 00:26:58,133
Very close attention.

568
00:27:06,224 --> 00:27:08,518
Oh, thank you. Please.
But that's just memory 101.

569
00:27:08,643 --> 00:27:11,479
Let's move forward
into the advanced coursework.

570
00:27:12,689 --> 00:27:13,815
For memory to be useful

571
00:27:13,940 --> 00:27:17,610
it has to be as accurate
and as fast as a camera.

572
00:27:17,736 --> 00:27:20,113
- How many animals did you get, Lisbon?
- Around 30.

573
00:27:20,280 --> 00:27:22,282
- And you got them from Linda's office?
- Yes, I did.

574
00:27:22,407 --> 00:27:24,701
All of the animals
came from Linda's office.

575
00:27:25,368 --> 00:27:27,287
That's a place
that I have never been.

576
00:27:27,620 --> 00:27:30,457
And in a moment,
I will turn around

577
00:27:30,623 --> 00:27:32,584
and look behind me...
and just for a few seconds...

578
00:27:32,709 --> 00:27:36,337
and I will memorize every animal
on those tables in order.

579
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:39,257
And then I will show you
how you can do the same.

580
00:27:39,716 --> 00:27:41,718
How do we know you didn't
already memorize them?

581
00:27:42,886 --> 00:27:45,680
A skeptic. I should have known.
And you're right.

582
00:27:46,723 --> 00:27:48,349
There's no reason
for you to trust me.

583
00:27:48,558 --> 00:27:52,729
Paul, would you mind rearranging
the animals any way you see fit?

584
00:27:53,104 --> 00:27:54,147
Sure.

585
00:27:55,148 --> 00:27:56,191
Thank you.

586
00:27:56,649 --> 00:27:57,901
The human brain.

587
00:27:58,985 --> 00:28:02,238
Once you get it working right,
it can be incredibly effective.

588
00:28:02,363 --> 00:28:05,158
The mind can do some
miraculous things.

589
00:28:05,867 --> 00:28:09,037
Uh, like...
telling the future.

590
00:28:09,329 --> 00:28:10,747
Which is what I'm doing
right now.

591
00:28:10,997 --> 00:28:12,040
Bull.

592
00:28:17,003 --> 00:28:18,046
For that...

593
00:28:20,590 --> 00:28:22,675
You can be the one
who holds the prediction.

594
00:28:23,218 --> 00:28:24,928
Science is about prediction,
isn't it?

595
00:28:26,179 --> 00:28:28,932
Predictions based on
hard data, not fortunetelling.

596
00:28:29,516 --> 00:28:31,226
Well, you say "tomato"

597
00:28:31,351 --> 00:28:33,853
I say scientists believe
in rational thought.

598
00:28:34,187 --> 00:28:36,189
And grids and lists
and classification

599
00:28:36,314 --> 00:28:38,066
which is fine, but it's not
how the world works

600
00:28:38,191 --> 00:28:40,026
and it's certainly not
how the brain works.

601
00:28:40,318 --> 00:28:42,987
You're saying
people aren't rational?

602
00:28:43,571 --> 00:28:48,451
Well, being rational is a tool
but it's not the only tool we have.

603
00:28:48,576 --> 00:28:51,913
Long before we stuffed our knowledge
into graphs and charts

604
00:28:52,622 --> 00:28:54,374
we used older and, in some cases

605
00:28:54,541 --> 00:28:57,418
more entertaining ways
to remember things.

606
00:28:57,669 --> 00:28:59,879
- How we doing there, Paul?
- I think we're good.

607
00:29:00,004 --> 00:29:01,506
Excellent. Please, take a seat.

608
00:29:01,756 --> 00:29:04,175
Now, I'd like to show you
what I'm talking about.

609
00:29:12,058 --> 00:29:14,018
Lisbon, uh, would you mind?

610
00:29:15,270 --> 00:29:16,312
Thank you.

611
00:29:17,856 --> 00:29:21,526
We can create a memory
by building a palace

612
00:29:22,277 --> 00:29:23,695
in our minds.

613
00:29:24,654 --> 00:29:26,406
We can sing ourselves a song

614
00:29:27,198 --> 00:29:28,700
or tell ourselves
a little story.

615
00:29:29,701 --> 00:29:31,828
A story of a bully...

616
00:29:32,287 --> 00:29:33,371
a gray fox...

617
00:29:33,913 --> 00:29:36,457
and a little brave man...
a rat...

618
00:29:36,916 --> 00:29:40,044
that stood up for his chubby
little buddy the toad

619
00:29:40,837 --> 00:29:42,463
and his friend egghead.

620
00:29:43,631 --> 00:29:47,343
The story of a man in a tuxedo
who snaked his way through a crowd

621
00:29:47,510 --> 00:29:49,262
to meet a woman
in a colorful dress.

622
00:29:50,221 --> 00:29:53,892
Penguin, cobra,
three squirrels, and a parrot.

623
00:29:54,767 --> 00:29:56,227
The story of flight...

624
00:29:56,477 --> 00:30:00,440
An owl, a bat, a moth, all...

625
00:30:02,942 --> 00:30:05,486
I sense I made a mistake.

626
00:30:05,778 --> 00:30:06,863
No moth.

627
00:30:08,573 --> 00:30:14,078
Well, perhaps my prediction
will explain my error.

628
00:30:14,329 --> 00:30:15,830
Uh, Jeanette,
could you read it out, please?

629
00:30:19,709 --> 00:30:23,296
- "The moth is in Paul's pocket"?
- Wait, what?

630
00:30:23,630 --> 00:30:24,672
The moth.

631
00:30:25,465 --> 00:30:27,008
The moth you stole
from the table

632
00:30:27,175 --> 00:30:28,676
when you were rearranging
the animals...

633
00:30:28,843 --> 00:30:30,094
it's in your pocket.

634
00:30:31,137 --> 00:30:32,889
I'm gonna need you to empty
your pockets.

635
00:30:33,306 --> 00:30:34,349
Come on.

636
00:30:36,059 --> 00:30:37,101
Let's have a look.

637
00:30:39,687 --> 00:30:41,189
Paul, why did you
take the moth?

638
00:30:42,690 --> 00:30:45,610
- That's a California Jade moth.
- So it is.

639
00:30:45,902 --> 00:30:46,945
The kind he discovered.

640
00:30:47,070 --> 00:30:48,905
I thought the only other one
was in the museum.

641
00:30:49,197 --> 00:30:50,240
Where did that come from?

642
00:30:50,573 --> 00:30:51,616
Wait a second.

643
00:30:51,741 --> 00:30:53,117
You said all the animals
came from Linda's office.

644
00:30:53,243 --> 00:30:54,285
They did.

645
00:30:55,495 --> 00:30:57,664
Linda died before
Paul discovered the moth.

646
00:30:57,872 --> 00:30:59,874
This moth couldn't have come
from her... office.

647
00:31:00,166 --> 00:31:01,209
You'll get there.

648
00:31:01,584 --> 00:31:04,170
Unless...
She discovered the moth first.

649
00:31:06,214 --> 00:31:07,257
Paul...

650
00:31:08,007 --> 00:31:10,468
- You didn't kill Linda.
- Yes, he did.

651
00:31:11,552 --> 00:31:13,972
But, uh, I confess...

652
00:31:14,138 --> 00:31:17,517
that moth didn't come
from Linda's office. I...

653
00:31:17,642 --> 00:31:21,396
S... well, I-I borrowed it
from the museum...

654
00:31:21,980 --> 00:31:23,147
to prove a point.

655
00:31:23,940 --> 00:31:24,983
Paul.

656
00:31:29,779 --> 00:31:31,197
I'd just had a paper rejected.

657
00:31:32,573 --> 00:31:34,200
Peer review
threw it back at me.

658
00:31:35,535 --> 00:31:37,203
Dr. Kidd was so disappointed.

659
00:31:37,954 --> 00:31:41,040
She never said anything.
She never had to.

660
00:31:41,249 --> 00:31:42,667
How did Linda come into it?

661
00:31:44,669 --> 00:31:46,004
"It was late night at the lab."

662
00:31:47,046 --> 00:31:50,633
"Uh, she came in fresh
from the forest, excited."

663
00:31:51,134 --> 00:31:53,553
"She'd seen one of her
woodpeckers eating a moth"

664
00:31:53,678 --> 00:31:54,887
"that she couldn't identify."

665
00:31:56,639 --> 00:31:58,224
So she put down some bait
and caught one.

666
00:31:59,642 --> 00:32:01,227
We found the brown sugar
in her office.

667
00:32:01,811 --> 00:32:03,354
- Was that her bait?
- Yeah.

668
00:32:04,689 --> 00:32:06,524
I knew what it was
the moment I saw it.

669
00:32:07,317 --> 00:32:08,651
"A new species."

670
00:32:09,569 --> 00:32:12,030
"I mean, that's articles
that's museum exhibits"

671
00:32:12,155 --> 00:32:13,656
that's money
for the program.

672
00:32:14,741 --> 00:32:16,409
I saw myself naming the moth.

673
00:32:17,702 --> 00:32:19,579
"I'd...I'd always wanted
to name something."

674
00:32:20,913 --> 00:32:22,123
"My job was on the line."

675
00:32:23,374 --> 00:32:24,417
"My future."

676
00:32:25,335 --> 00:32:26,377
"I needed it..."

677
00:32:27,337 --> 00:32:28,379
To survive.

678
00:32:38,848 --> 00:32:40,975
I just lost myself completely.

679
00:32:42,852 --> 00:32:45,897
"I came back to my senses.
I cleaned up."

680
00:32:46,814 --> 00:32:48,608
"Lots of ways to clean up blood in the lab."

681
00:32:49,525 --> 00:32:52,987
"And then, I gave Linda to the warms."

682
00:33:06,959 --> 00:33:09,462
Then I went out to the part of the forest
where I knew she'd been.

683
00:33:11,381 --> 00:33:12,965
Discovered the moth.

684
00:33:15,093 --> 00:33:16,761
I came back
and got my accolades.

685
00:33:18,346 --> 00:33:19,639
I saved my job.

686
00:33:21,849 --> 00:33:22,892
I got everything I wanted.

687
00:33:37,115 --> 00:33:38,157
Dr. Kidd.

688
00:33:40,243 --> 00:33:42,245
Sonia, please.

689
00:33:43,162 --> 00:33:45,206
I wanted to thank you
for catching Paul.

690
00:33:46,499 --> 00:33:48,918
There's no thanks necessary.

691
00:33:51,838 --> 00:33:54,382
Linda's dead, Paul's in jail

692
00:33:54,507 --> 00:33:56,551
and Megan is being expelled.

693
00:33:57,468 --> 00:33:59,303
I don't think the program
will ever recover.

694
00:34:00,054 --> 00:34:01,264
The program isn't real.

695
00:34:02,723 --> 00:34:04,475
The question is,
will you recover?

696
00:34:07,061 --> 00:34:08,312
I felt so powerless.

697
00:34:09,313 --> 00:34:11,149
I mean, what can you do
in the face of death?

698
00:34:13,317 --> 00:34:15,987
You're not powerless, though.
You act.

699
00:34:17,029 --> 00:34:18,156
Well, there's one thing
you can do.

700
00:34:18,364 --> 00:34:22,368
Uh... Paul took a lot of pride
in naming that moth, when...

701
00:34:22,660 --> 00:34:24,245
When it should have been
Linda who named it.

702
00:34:24,370 --> 00:34:25,413
Yes.

703
00:34:25,913 --> 00:34:28,124
I'll make sure to rename it
after her.

704
00:34:29,208 --> 00:34:30,251
Thank you.

705
00:34:30,835 --> 00:34:31,878
Pleasure.

706
00:34:37,967 --> 00:34:39,010
Yeah.

707
00:34:40,094 --> 00:34:41,220
Makes sense.

708
00:34:41,762 --> 00:34:44,182
- All the good ones are taken.
- Please.

709
00:34:44,348 --> 00:34:46,934
- Natural selection, I suppose.
- Come on.

710
00:34:48,728 --> 00:34:50,021
Good-bye, Patrick.

711
00:34:50,354 --> 00:34:51,397
Good-bye, Sonia.

712
00:35:00,990 --> 00:35:02,325
$500 to Dawkins.

713
00:35:04,035 --> 00:35:05,077
Nope.

714
00:35:07,997 --> 00:35:09,040
I'll, uh...

715
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:12,043
Raise $100.

716
00:35:14,003 --> 00:35:16,130
Well, I'm just gonna have to
raise you another $100.

717
00:35:18,257 --> 00:35:19,425
When are you gonna learn, Gale?

718
00:35:26,015 --> 00:35:27,058
Well...

719
00:35:29,977 --> 00:35:31,020
Raise $1,000.

720
00:35:35,358 --> 00:35:36,400
All in.

721
00:35:54,710 --> 00:35:55,753
Two pair.

722
00:36:03,928 --> 00:36:05,721
Flush.

723
00:36:06,681 --> 00:36:08,474
Flush takes the pot.

724
00:36:13,771 --> 00:36:14,814
"Goodnight Moon."

725
00:36:17,483 --> 00:36:18,901
"Goodnight Stars."

726
00:36:20,486 --> 00:36:21,529
"Goodnight Judge."

727
00:36:28,286 --> 00:36:29,745
You see that?
Did you see that?

728
00:36:30,162 --> 00:36:31,330
The flush?
It was very nice.

729
00:36:31,455 --> 00:36:32,873
Ah, I gutted him!

730
00:36:33,332 --> 00:36:35,209
I would say so, yeah.

731
00:36:35,376 --> 00:36:37,837
That was very slick,
you having Jane give me lessons.

732
00:36:38,379 --> 00:36:40,631
You must really want to help
Van Pelt.

733
00:36:40,756 --> 00:36:41,882
What did Jane do?

734
00:36:42,049 --> 00:36:43,884
Oh, now there's no need
to be coy.

735
00:36:44,302 --> 00:36:46,429
I don't have a problem
with a little quid pro quo.

736
00:36:47,388 --> 00:36:49,473
The White Hat program,
wasn't it?

737
00:36:50,433 --> 00:36:52,059
I'll authorize the stipend
in the morning.

738
00:36:52,768 --> 00:36:54,729
- Thank you, sir.
- No, no. Thank you.

739
00:36:55,313 --> 00:36:57,273
- And thank Jane.
- I will.

740
00:37:04,780 --> 00:37:09,160
And some scientists think the T. Rex
could run up to 45 miles an hour.

741
00:37:09,452 --> 00:37:10,494
That's apex predator.

742
00:37:10,620 --> 00:37:11,704
- You know what that means?
- Yes.

743
00:37:11,912 --> 00:37:13,080
